5. BUGS FOUND AND FIXED - THE MOST IMPORTANT SECTION
--------------------------------------------------------------------------------
Six bugs were found, four of which changed results. All but one were caught by
tests or by a study failing for the wrong reason, not by reading code.

B1-1  THE MINI-ULTIMATUM'S NO-ALTERNATIVE CONDITION WAS GENERATED ZERO TIMES.
      Its four alternatives - (5,5), (2,8), (10,0) and no alternative - are
      experimental CONDITIONS, not reward levels. Applying the plan's mod-4
      split rule to them put each in exactly one split and the no-alternative
      condition in the LOCKED TEST SPLIT, so the contrast the design exists to
      measure was absent from the dataset entirely.
      Fix: conditions are replicated across splits; only reward levels are
      divided. (BD5)

B1-2  THE TRANSFER BATTERY WAS CUT TO A QUARTER.
      Splitting Holt-Laury by item index left 3 of 10 decisions in disc, and a
      ladder with three rungs has no switching point - the instrument measures
      nothing. Fix: each instrument is emitted whole within each split. (BD5)

B1-3  SOCIAL TRIALS SCORED FAIRNESS AS A DOMINANCE VIOLATION.
      Accepting always pays a responder at least as much as rejecting, so
      marking every monetary shortfall as dominated would have counted every
      inequity-averse rejection - the preference under study - as an attention
      failure. Fix: dominance is recorded only on catch trials, where by
      construction no preference explains the choice.

B1-4  LABEL SETS WERE APPLIED ACROSS DOMAINS. (BD13)
      expand_formats defaulted to EVERY label set in LABEL_SETS, so once the
      social pairs were added it dressed lottery trials in Reject/Accept and
      Yes/No, producing prompts that read:
          Reject: $20 for sure
          Accept: a 10% chance of $60 and otherwise nothing
      Three-quarters of the risk grid carried a label pair that does not
      describe the choice, and the label-bias term was computed against that.
      DOMAIN_LABEL_SETS existed but was never wired in. The expanded grid fell
      from 29,408 rows to the correct 14,704. The first B1 dataset and the first
      two B2 submissions were built on the wrong grid and were rebuilt.

B2-1  TEMPLATE EFFECTS WERE REDRAWN PER CONDITION. (BD11)
      A template effect is a property of the TEMPLATE, so a real experiment that
      runs baseline and intervention on the same prompts sees the same offset in
      both and it cancels in the comparison. Redrawing it put the difference of
      two independent draws into every trial as a CONSTANT SHIFT - which is
      exactly the signature of an action bias - so a genuine preference shift at
      the MDC was being attributed to action. The symptom was seed dependence
      that should not have existed: on one seed pref_alpha gave a preference
      share of 0.996, on another an action share of 0.89. With the draw shared,
      the true model wins in both. Only the numerical-noise component is
      redrawn per condition, which is correct.

B2-2  THE BELIEF DISTORTION HAD EXACTLY ZERO EFFECT.
      Scaling every amount by kappa multiplies both the utility difference and
      the contextual utility range by kappa^alpha, so it cancels and cannot
      change any behaviour. Fix: the distortion applies to the GAMBLE's x, which
      is what B1 means by x, and not to the sure amount s.

B0-1  free(model) DELETED A LOCAL NAME, NOT THE CALLER'S.
      Passing the model to a helper and deleting the parameter there drops only
      that helper's local reference; the caller still held it, so 282 GB of fp32
      weights stayed resident and the bf16 load OOMed. Fix: one process per
      dtype, so the operating system does the freeing.

THREE FURTHER ESTIMATOR CORRECTIONS (BD14)
The first full recovery run failed Gate B2 in all 18 configurations with EVERY
agent, preference shifts included, classified as not preference-like. A rule
that rejects everything has perfect specificity for free and is not a rule, so
the failure was diagnosed rather than reported:
  (a) THE MODEL HAD NO TEMPLATE TERM. B1 gives each split two templates and the
      plan asks B2 to simulate template variation of 0.5-2 logits; with no
      per-template intercept that variation is unmodelled misfit, and the
      comparison between nested models is decided by which one best absorbs it.
      Adding intercepts raised the preference share for a true alpha shift from
      0.265 to 0.591. They are not separately identified - templates are nested
      within splits, so a template effect is confounded with that split's reward
      levels - and only differences matter; the first is pinned at zero.
  (b) THE FIT USED BINARY CHOICES WHEN THE INSTRUMENT RETURNS z. The plan's own
      diagnostics are written on z and dz and the logit readout returns z
      directly; fitting one Bernoulli draw per row discards most of the
      measurement. With a Gaussian observation mode on z, alpha's RMSE fell from
      0.055 to 0.0054 and gamma's to 0.0032, and the preference share for a true
      shift went to 1.000 with the action model rejected in every draw. Both
      modes are kept: "choice" is what sampled generation gives, "logit" what
      the readout gives.
  (c) D5's TRANSFER RATIO DIVIDED TWO SQUASHED PROBABILITY DIFFERENCES. A change
      in choice probability is a few points wide, so the ratio of two of them
      swung outside the pre-registered band on noise alone and failed genuine
      preference shifts about half the time. It now uses mean dz, like the
      plan's other diagnostics, and a zero effect in both formats is scored as
      perfect transfer rather than as a ratio of 0/0.

NO THRESHOLD WAS EVER TOUCHED. Plan v4's numbers live in one place in
decision.py and a test (test_gate_thresholds_are_plan_v4) asserts them.

